#!/usr/bin/env perl
# roc(), auc() and auroc() against pROC and scikit-learn.
#
# pROC 1.19.0.1 (R 4.6.1).  The data is pROC's own aSAH, the 113 patients its
# tests/testthat/ suite pins its values on, and t/roc.R.R regenerates the
# block between BEGIN GENERATED and END GENERATED below (it says how).  The
# numbers it writes are the ones pROC's tests assert:
#   test-auc.R    "full auc works": auc(r.wfns) 0.823678861788618,
#                 auc(r.ndka) 0.611957994579946
#   test-var.R    var(r.wfns) 0.00146991470882363, var(r.ndka)
#                 0.0031908105493913, var(r.s100b) 0.00266868245717244
#                 (the squares of the se below)
#   test-ci.auc.R "ci.auc with delong works": ci.auc(r.ndka)
#   test-roc.R    "roc.default handles NAs": ndka[1:20] <- NA with
#                 na.rm = TRUE gives the AUC of rows 21:113
# plus each curve's (sensitivity, specificity) from coords(r, "all"), and
# var() of a one-case curve, which pROC answers with NA.  pROC's direction
# "<" (controls < cases) is Stats::LikeR's direction '>'.  pROC places its
# thresholds between the observed values and walks them upwards; roc() uses
# the values themselves and walks downwards, so its curve is pROC's reversed,
# point for point.
#
# scikit-learn 1.9.1, sklearn/metrics/tests/test_ranking.py,
# test_roc_curve_toydata: the expected fpr/tpr and AUC of each two-point
# case, copied as literals.  That test unpacks "tpr, fpr, _ = roc_curve(...)"
# although roc_curve() returns (fpr, tpr, thresholds), so its "tpr" is the
# false-positive rate; the arrays here are named for what they hold.
# roc_curve(drop_intermediate = False) uses the same thresholds as roc() --
# the distinct scores, highest first, after a leading +Inf.
#
# Deliberate divergences:
#   - A curve with one class croaks here; sklearn warns and returns NaN.
#   - +-Inf scores are ordinary extreme values here; pROC returns NaN for
#     the whole curve and sklearn raises.
#
# Tolerances.  Against pROC, on perl-5.44.0 (double), the AUC, the CI and
# every sensitivity and specificity agreed exactly, and the DeLong se to
# 1.34e-16 relative (one ulp).  1e-13 relative leaves room for long-double
# and quadmath builds, where the frozen literal is itself only a double.
